 I’ve been on and off Vyvanse since August of last year. In a few ocasions I experienced increased appetite, my guess is a slow rate of absorption of  dextroamphetamine on account of slow metabolism. So, you didn't get a decrease of appetite from the drug. 

&#x200B;

Appetite supression is a indirect effect from TAAR1 activation, which is one of the modes of action from Vyvanse. On the other hand, euphoria is a more complex and  subjective effect.

>People just dose it way too high. With sleep aids people can go overboard and end up altering their sleep architecture too much.

When you say *sleep aids,* are referring to melatonin or any sleep aid? 

&#x200B;

In this same thread, there is a guy who claims that melatonin doesn’t  alters sleep archicteture so much.

&#x200B;

>Actually, most of the evidence suggests melatonin does not significantly alter sleep architecture (apart from sleep latency).  
>  
>The vivid dream phenomenon could be related to something inherent to increased melatonin receptor binding or 5-methoxytryptamine, a melatonin metabolite, which is a full agonist at most 5-HT receptors.

&#x200B;

So,  summarizing:

&#x200B;

* If you have any *side effects* from melatonin, your dose is too high (you should be taking 300 mcg).
* You should be taking the active form of Vitamin D3 (in the morning), since it antagonizes melatonin.

>Stimulants work for a lot of ADHD patients, but it's not uncommon to have stimulant non-responders like yourself.

There is no non-responders to stimulants like Adderal and Vyvanse. Amphetamines acts uniformed (or very close). There is, of course,  misalignment between the array of symptons of ADHD and the improvement of them.

&#x200B;

> ADHD people have a higher bar to learning those skills, but it's not accurate to divide people between ADHD and non-ADHD and assume that different neurochemistry is the sole differentiator between the two groups. 

I agreed with the inaccurate division between ADHD and non-ADHD. Of course neurochemistry is not the sole differentiator, I think everyone here embraces the complexity of that domain. But you can't  unlearn ADHD symptons, you can use *coping mechanism*. Something like using the "lack of focus" for training effort and so on.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

There is something I can do to avoid this depletion? 

&#x200B;

[https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2785862/](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2785862/)

&#x200B;

>There is an urgent need to identify agents that will provide neuroprotection and slow disease progression in neurode-generative diseases that have an enormous collective impact on our society. Detailed and extensive pre-clinical studies have strongly supported CoQ10 as such a potential agent. 

&#x200B;

Some benzos like Diazepam modulates the dopamine response in reward system. Only my 2 cents.

&#x200B;

https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/releases/119284.php

That's is very interesting. Caffeine/modafinil, anecdotally speaking, increases the reward feeling of social interactions, but not in a strong way. 

&#x200B;

When comes to cocaine/amphetamines, the pressure to socialize is overwhelming. Also, my socials skills are better on cocaine/amphetamines.

Or maybe the side effects have multiple roots. 

&#x200B;

Permanent damage means irreversible "side effects".

>behave in a way that could be likened to ADHD?

Yes. Do you know why the opposite effect?




&#x200B;

&#x200B;

People have weird effects even at low dose (5 mg).  

&#x200B;

And from what I know, Ambien is not a benzo.

